# Gateway Capture Test Repo — Build Brief

## Context

This is a standalone test repo, separate from the Hangar project. Its job is to
capture real request/reply traffic from Lockheed's Genesis gateway, which
Hangar's "oracle" component needs before it can finish building its model
adapter. Nothing here needs to match Hangar's code — it just needs to produce
raw, faithful captures that get handed back separately.

**Build one piece at a time.** After each piece below, stop and let me test it
before moving to the next. Don't combine steps or get ahead of what's been
verified working.

## Background the session should know

- Genesis gateway has two paths:
  - `/v1` — OpenAI-style Responses API. Used for GPT-OSS-120b (on-prem, free)
    via `model_provider=aifactory`, and also hosts external models like
    Terra 5.6 on the same endpoint.
  - `/api/anthropic` — native Anthropic Messages API format. Used for Claude
    models.
- On-prem vs. external is a property of the *model*, not the endpoint — the US
  endpoint hosts both on-prem and external models.
- Cache-related usage fields differ by path:
  - `/v1` path: `cached_input_tokens`, `cache_write_input_tokens`
  - `/api/anthropic` path: `cache_creation_input_tokens`, `cache_read_input_tokens`
- Credentials come from the shell environment already set up on this machine
  (same pattern as existing Codex/Claude CLI aliases) — no credential handling
  needs to be built here.

## What "done" looks like, overall

A set of captured exchanges, saved as plain files, covering:
1. GPT-OSS-120b (on-prem) — full tool-call chain
2. Terra 5.6 (external, same US endpoint) — full tool-call chain
3. Claude (via `/api/anthropic`) — full tool-call chain
4. At least one reply in the set showing cache activity in usage data

Each captured exchange must preserve, exactly as sent/received:
- Method and path
- Every header (auth token replaced with an obvious placeholder, but left in
  its original header position)
- Body, byte-for-byte, no reformatting or key reordering
- Response status line and headers
- Whether the response was streamed/chunked

No predefined file format is required — a plain directory of files is fine,
one exchange per file or request/reply as a pair. Keep the tool-call sequence
reconstructable as an ordered chain, not just loose files.

## Build order (one step at a time)

### Step 1 — Raw HTTP client, single call, GPT-OSS-120b
Build a minimal script that sends one hand-built request to `/v1` for
GPT-OSS-120b (on-prem, free to iterate on) and saves the raw request and raw
response to disk, unmodified except for token redaction. No tool calling yet
— just prove the raw send/save mechanics work end to end.

**Stop here for testing.**

### Step 2 — Add a forced tool call, GPT-OSS-120b
Extend Step 1's script so the request forces a tool call (e.g. a simple file
listing tool). Capture the model's tool-call request. Don't send a tool result
back yet — just confirm the tool-call request itself is captured correctly.

**Stop here for testing.**

### Step 3 — Complete the chain, GPT-OSS-120b
Add sending a fabricated tool result back to the model and capturing its final
reply. This completes a full request → tool-call → tool-result → final-reply
chain for GPT-OSS-120b.

**Stop here for testing.**

### Step 4 — Point the same client at Terra 5.6
Reuse Step 3's chain-driving logic, but target Terra 5.6 (external model, same
`/v1` endpoint, same US gateway). Capture the same kind of chain. Compare the
reply shape against GPT-OSS-120b's — specifically checking for any
cost/billing-related fields that weren't present in the on-prem reply.

**Stop here for testing.**

### Step 5 — Raw HTTP client for Claude path
Build a new raw client (separate from the `/v1` one, since the wire format is
different) targeting `/api/anthropic`. Start with a single call, no tool
calling yet, same save-raw-exactly-as-is approach as Step 1.

**Stop here for testing.**

### Step 6 — Full tool-call chain, Claude path
Extend Step 5 to run the same request → tool-call → tool-result → final-reply
sequence as Steps 2–3, on the Claude path this time. This is the path with
weaker prior evidence, so expect surprises here more than on the GPT-OSS side.

**Stop here for testing.**

### Step 7 — Confirm cache-activity coverage
Review the captured set from Steps 1–6. Confirm at least one reply shows
nonzero cache-related usage fields (either path). If none do, figure out what
triggers cache activity (e.g. a repeated/similar prompt) and capture one that
does.

**Stop here for testing.**

### Step 8 — Pricing data (separate, lower priority)
Only after the above is solid: look at whatever Genesis webpage lists models
and prices. Try scraping its HTML/DOM directly first. Only fall back to
screenshot + vision-model extraction if the page has no usable structured
data. Either way, write output to a separate data file — not into any
oracle/Hangar code file directly — and flag any model-read values for manual
review before trusting them.

## What NOT to do

- Don't build a fixture loader or try to match Hangar's expected format —
  that doesn't exist yet by design; Hangar's coordinator builds the loader
  after seeing what this repo produces.
- Don't redact anything beyond the auth token.
- Don't reformat or "clean up" captured JSON — save it exactly as received.
